00:05:54.640 --> 00:05:55.210
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:05:55.210 --> 00:05:58.915
For that winter term internship,
was that part of a program or

00:05:58.915 --> 00:06:02.560
was that something that you
kind of pursued independently?

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:05.010
Like how did you go about that?

00:06:05.010 --> 00:06:05.760
&gt;&gt; Yeah, it's a good question.

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:09.060
So I would say,
it ended up being a little bit of both.

00:06:09.060 --> 00:06:11.470
I'm trying to remember
where I found out about it.

00:06:11.470 --> 00:06:16.408
I believe it had been posted by either
one of my classmates through a friend of

00:06:16.408 --> 00:06:21.037
a friend, or it was on like the careers
to the career website sort of like

00:06:21.037 --> 00:06:22.755
routinely update stuff.

00:06:22.755 --> 00:06:26.782
And it's the stuff that they update
is not only for full time or

00:06:26.782 --> 00:06:31.280
official internship stuff,
some of it is a little bit more ad-hoc.

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:35.715
So it was a resource that I used pretty
extensively throughout my time to kind of

00:06:35.715 --> 00:06:39.830
see like, hey, every few days like,
is there something interesting?

00:06:39.830 --> 00:06:43.149
It's a fairly like low cost
effort to hop on the website and

00:06:43.149 --> 00:06:45.260
see if there's interesting stuff.

00:06:45.260 --> 00:06:49.393
Of course, I'd say, pursuing a part
time internship during school

00:06:49.393 --> 00:06:54.096
is the major like, decision just because
of all the different things vying for

00:06:54.096 --> 00:06:57.387
your time when you're at the GSB,
which are awesome.

00:06:57.387 --> 00:06:59.788
And you normally have to trade stuff off,

00:06:59.788 --> 00:07:03.070
you can't do everything
despite our desire to.

00:07:03.070 --> 00:07:04.768
So I think I, now that I think of it,

00:07:04.768 --> 00:07:07.450
I probably found out about
it from that career website.

00:07:09.110 --> 00:07:12.880
&gt;&gt; And you already like started to
go into what I was about to ask you.

00:07:12.880 --> 00:07:17.595
How did you balance both that
internship plus like winter quarter of

00:07:17.595 --> 00:07:18.926
your MBA one year?

00:07:18.926 --> 00:07:22.848
Didn't you still have like some
of your core requirements?

00:07:22.848 --> 00:07:23.850
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:07:23.850 --> 00:07:28.950
So I think, winter quarter freshman year,
MBA one year is known

00:07:28.950 --> 00:07:33.862
as like one of the more brutal
academic quarters because you're,

00:07:33.862 --> 00:07:38.660
usually finances the kind of
killer class in that set.

00:07:38.660 --> 00:07:40.680
And yeah, it was not a,

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:46.740
I think the internship ended up
devoting about ten to 12 hours a week.

00:07:46.740 --> 00:07:50.940
So it wasn't crazy but again,
it was up in San Francisco.

00:07:50.940 --> 00:07:56.750
So I was taking, Wednesdays, we normally
like don't have classes at the GSB.

00:07:56.750 --> 00:08:00.850
So it was a good opportunity to head up.

00:08:00.850 --> 00:08:05.906
To the city and do mostly a full day in
the office during that I would usually do

00:08:05.906 --> 00:08:10.821
kind of half a day either on Fridays or
on Mondays with whatever or without.

00:08:10.821 --> 00:08:15.632
It was not easy and there's a lot of,
I would say jealousy

00:08:15.632 --> 00:08:20.754
seeing what other classmates of
mine were doing at the time.

00:08:20.754 --> 00:08:24.894
But I felt like it was still worth
investigating especially prior

00:08:24.894 --> 00:08:28.972
to my internship maybe kind of
full time internship first year.

00:08:28.972 --> 00:08:33.437
So because I want to say, okay, this seems
like the right route, it doesn't seem like

00:08:33.437 --> 00:08:37.150
the right route, in this case it
didn't seem like the right route.

00:08:37.150 --> 00:08:40.270
So I think it was time well spent.

00:08:40.270 --> 00:08:44.220
Yeah, and then kind of sticking
on this theme of internships.

00:08:44.220 --> 00:08:48.300
So, you did two internships in the summer
between your MBA one and MBA two year?

00:08:49.490 --> 00:08:51.470
No, no, so I did that one in the winter.

00:08:51.470 --> 00:08:54.310
I didn't do what we call like a GSD.

00:08:54.310 --> 00:08:59.165
I just did one, I did a 12 week
internship at PayPal between my first and

00:08:59.165 --> 00:09:00.970
second years.

00:09:00.970 --> 00:09:07.410
Okay, and then there's a question here
about how early does recruiting start for

00:09:07.410 --> 00:09:11.720
project management internships
at some of the firms.

00:09:11.720 --> 00:09:13.760
Do you have any insight
there because- Yeah, so

00:09:13.760 --> 00:09:17.750
it really depends how big of a company
you're looking to intern at.

00:09:17.750 --> 00:09:22.485
So the general trend that you'll
see is that the large companies

00:09:22.485 --> 00:09:26.258
that have very organized,
headcount modeling and

00:09:26.258 --> 00:09:31.170
recruiting processes are able to
kick off that process actually,

00:09:31.170 --> 00:09:35.906
towards the end of November you
start chit chatting with them and

00:09:35.906 --> 00:09:37.940
seeing what's out there.

00:09:37.940 --> 00:09:42.703
And then interviews will actually begin
around the middle of January when you

00:09:42.703 --> 00:09:45.790
return from winter break
heading into February.

00:09:45.790 --> 00:09:50.637
That said, I see the majority of people
that are looking to go into tech

00:09:50.637 --> 00:09:54.579
end up getting their internships
in the spring quarter,

00:09:54.579 --> 00:09:57.620
which is between April and in June.

00:09:57.620 --> 00:10:01.839
And that is usually smaller companies, but

00:10:01.839 --> 00:10:06.628
even some of the larger
companies kind of come from

00:10:06.628 --> 00:10:11.430
through with opportunities
later on in the year.

00:10:11.430 --> 00:10:13.548
I think one thing that's helpful and

00:10:13.548 --> 00:10:16.892
coming in is sort of having
a hypothesis of whether or not,

00:10:16.892 --> 00:10:20.940
a large company is of interest,
it's a small company is of interest.

00:10:20.940 --> 00:10:26.500
Both, because that'll sort of define
the timeline that you end up taking.

00:10:26.500 --> 00:10:29.587
Yeah, and so like were recruiters
coming to campus where you,

00:10:29.587 --> 00:10:33.432
did you do some initial reach out or how
did you make some of these connections or

00:10:33.432 --> 00:10:35.320
to learn about the opportunities?

00:10:35.320 --> 00:10:36.119
Yeah, it's a great question.

00:10:36.119 --> 00:10:40.576
So, I started with, again,
the lowest friction approach,

00:10:40.576 --> 00:10:42.731
which is the career website.

00:10:42.731 --> 00:10:48.520
So that was, again, the easiest place to
access, I think it was after the six week.

00:10:48.520 --> 00:10:51.100
I can't even remember what it's called.

00:10:51.100 --> 00:10:55.873
But there's a bit of a moratorium on
career stuff during the first six weeks,

00:10:55.873 --> 00:11:00.355
which is actually great to let people
settle into academics on campus for

00:11:00.355 --> 00:11:02.160
your MBA one year.

00:11:02.160 --> 00:11:04.690
And then everything sort of opens up.

00:11:04.690 --> 00:11:09.290
And so I recall,
checking out the career website a bit.

00:11:09.290 --> 00:11:13.950
And then, starting to develop my own list.

00:11:13.950 --> 00:11:17.732
There are a bunch of other resources
if you're more focused on.

00:11:17.732 --> 00:11:22.553
Smaller companies if you're
focused on specific niches within

00:11:22.553 --> 00:11:27.295
the tech sector, tech can stand for
a lot of different things.

00:11:27.295 --> 00:11:31.156
And then obviously you start
kind of poking at alumni and

00:11:31.156 --> 00:11:37.073
the alumni directory seeing what kind of
random emails come through from classmate,

00:11:37.073 --> 00:11:39.634
who used to work at these companies.

00:11:39.634 --> 00:11:44.209
You start doing a lot, a lot of coffee
chats with MBA twos who're usually

00:11:44.209 --> 00:11:47.690
really like nice and
generous with their time.

00:11:47.690 --> 00:11:51.892
So, it's a whole host of things and that's
kind of what I was getting out with,

00:11:51.892 --> 00:11:54.354
time is at a premium for
everything at the GSB.

00:11:54.354 --> 00:12:00.900
And so being a little bit thrifty with
how you spend it, is really critical.

00:12:00.900 --> 00:12:05.099
Yeah, and then as you're thinking about,
so your journey and

00:12:05.099 --> 00:12:10.091
then other friends or folks that you know
who have gone into the tech space or

00:12:10.091 --> 00:12:12.248
the product management space.

00:12:12.248 --> 00:12:19.005
There's a question here about, do you have
to have a background in product management

00:12:19.005 --> 00:12:24.720
to get an internship or a job after or
kind of, how does that look like?

00:12:24.720 --> 00:12:25.510
That's a good question.

00:12:25.510 --> 00:12:29.526
I think it's a pretty common question
that a lot of my classmates came in with

00:12:29.526 --> 00:12:30.039
as well.

00:12:30.039 --> 00:12:33.911
And definitely they are concerned because
a lot of people come in with a sort of

00:12:33.911 --> 00:12:37.200
an HR hypothesis saying this
product thing sounds interesting.

00:12:37.200 --> 00:12:42.010
I've done a bit of research,
but can I get in?

00:12:42.010 --> 00:12:46.317
I definitely have a lot of classmates who
did not have product experience who were

00:12:46.317 --> 00:12:48.620
able to get into product.

00:12:48.620 --> 00:12:51.449
The way that I usually look at it,

00:12:51.449 --> 00:12:56.109
with product is sort of what
strengths you can play on.

00:12:56.109 --> 00:13:02.850
And prior experience and background is
a facet but definitely not the only facet.

00:13:02.850 --> 00:13:07.529
So other sort of pillars to think
about when you're considering this

00:13:07.529 --> 00:13:12.500
sort of career would be one like I
think attention to detail is critical.

00:13:12.500 --> 00:13:16.507
I think the ability to
think strategically,

00:13:16.507 --> 00:13:22.000
especially as future MBA graduates,
is a huge one to play up.

00:13:22.000 --> 00:13:26.520
And that's something that companies will
really be looking for, being analytical.

00:13:26.520 --> 00:13:29.690
So a lot of folks that have
consulting experience or

00:13:29.690 --> 00:13:34.940
finance experience can sort of leverage
that and play that up in their interview.

00:13:34.940 --> 00:13:38.571
Another critical facet that
people are looking for

00:13:38.571 --> 00:13:42.547
when it relates to product,
is interpersonal skills.

00:13:42.547 --> 00:13:45.891
Product managers end up being
in the middle of a whole bunch

00:13:45.891 --> 00:13:48.410
of different functions at their company.

00:13:48.410 --> 00:13:52.749
And for better or worse, your key to
success is being able to communicate

00:13:52.749 --> 00:13:56.378
with your other team members who
are not product managers and

00:13:56.378 --> 00:13:58.530
sort of speaking their languages.

00:13:58.530 --> 00:14:02.897
So, some of the, what we call soft skills,

00:14:02.897 --> 00:14:09.170
that we focus on a lot at the GSB
end up really helping with that.

00:14:09.170 --> 00:14:12.321
So I would say as far as recruiting goes,

00:14:12.321 --> 00:14:18.452
you have to strategically understand
what company you're interviewing on.

00:14:18.452 --> 00:14:21.891
Who you're talking to, and
what strengths you can play up.

00:14:21.891 --> 00:14:24.699
It won't be the same sort of script or

00:14:24.699 --> 00:14:29.773
story that you can tell it every
company it really will depend on how

00:14:29.773 --> 00:14:34.867
you can convince that you have
the right skill set to bring to them.

00:14:34.867 --> 00:14:37.967
And given the specific team
that you're interviewing at,

00:14:37.967 --> 00:14:40.385
the specific industry
that the company is at,

00:14:40.385 --> 00:14:44.601
but you definitely will have both from
your PGSB experience, MGSB experience,

00:14:44.601 --> 00:14:48.710
a bunch of tools in your toolkit to
talk about during the interview process.

00:14:48.710 --> 00:14:55.347
Even if you don't have more classic
like I was a PM prior to the GSB.

00:14:55.347 --> 00:14:56.130
Perfect segue.

00:14:56.130 --> 00:14:58.320
Let's talk about some of these tools.

00:14:58.320 --> 00:15:01.895
I know on one side there
are some of the hard skills and

00:15:01.895 --> 00:15:05.961
then you mentioned some of
the softer skills interpersonal

00:15:05.961 --> 00:15:09.470
kind of elements that are so
critical to your work.

00:15:09.470 --> 00:15:13.039
Can you tell me a little
bit about any classes?

00:15:13.039 --> 00:15:16.593
Let's start with the soft skill side,
the interpersonal,

00:15:16.593 --> 00:15:19.510
anything that stood out to you and why.

00:15:19.510 --> 00:15:25.887
Of course, I mean, I think for the very,
we joke about this as GSB graduates,

00:15:25.887 --> 00:15:30.721
but the class that you will
often hear about is colloquially

00:15:30.721 --> 00:15:34.799
called touchy feely or
interpersonal dynamics.

00:15:34.799 --> 00:15:35.846
And I think for

00:15:35.846 --> 00:15:41.179
me it was a class that I heard about
coming free prior to coming to the GSB.

00:15:41.179 --> 00:15:44.185
And it definitely did not disappoint.

00:15:44.185 --> 00:15:49.407
[LAUGH] And it's really interesting
experiential class where you spend

00:15:49.407 --> 00:15:54.279
the majority of your time rather than,
like in a lecture setting,

00:15:54.279 --> 00:15:58.542
you end up spending it with
what's called a tea group which

00:15:58.542 --> 00:16:04.120
is a group of you plus 11 of your
classmates plus the facilitator.

00:16:04.120 --> 00:16:09.336
And you go through a bunch of, call it,
interpersonal journey [LAUGH] for

00:16:09.336 --> 00:16:12.926
a quarter and
that was really critical, I think,

00:16:12.926 --> 00:16:16.618
for understanding how
other people perceive you.

00:16:16.618 --> 00:16:21.574
How different things that you end
up saying really touch other people

00:16:21.574 --> 00:16:25.160
even if you don't necessarily mean them.

00:16:25.160 --> 00:16:29.634
And then kind of if you want to think
about it more about how do I get

00:16:29.634 --> 00:16:30.570
my job done?

00:16:30.570 --> 00:16:32.565
It's also how you can influence people.

00:16:32.565 --> 00:16:36.455
Ultimately, a lot of this stuff is about,
especially when it comes to product,

00:16:36.455 --> 00:16:37.558
is around influence.

00:16:37.558 --> 00:16:42.327
How can you influence people to believe
in the the problems that you're

00:16:42.327 --> 00:16:45.280
trying to prioritize as a product manager?

00:16:45.280 --> 00:16:50.450
And so that class touchy-feely,
your interpersonal dynamics,

00:16:50.450 --> 00:16:53.598
is definitely a critical one, I think.

00:16:53.598 --> 00:16:57.152
There are a few other classes that I think
are mentioned less often that are around

00:16:57.152 --> 00:16:57.789
soft skills.

00:16:57.789 --> 00:17:02.729
So for example,
one is called acting with power which is

00:17:02.729 --> 00:17:06.626
a really fun one, where you literally act.

00:17:06.626 --> 00:17:14.144
[LAUGH] It is a very real acting, which
for me was not a point of comfort acting,

00:17:14.144 --> 00:17:18.078
not something I have background in, but

00:17:18.078 --> 00:17:25.270
it was a really fun unique experience
that you can only achieve in school.

00:17:25.270 --> 00:17:29.193
I think it's very difficult to
replicate elsewhere in the workplace.

00:17:29.193 --> 00:17:34.960
And we actually would have scripts
slash academic materials explaining

00:17:34.960 --> 00:17:40.547
how different tactics cause people
to perceive us in different ways.

00:17:40.547 --> 00:17:45.283
And it sounds very ego maniacal but
it can go a long way when

00:17:45.283 --> 00:17:50.770
you're in different sort of
situations in the workplace.

00:17:50.770 --> 00:17:53.345
So those are some of the softer ones,
will do,

00:17:53.345 --> 00:17:56.630
I'm going to transition to
some more tangible things.

00:17:56.630 --> 00:17:58.190
&gt;&gt; No,
I actually have a couple more questions.

00:17:58.190 --> 00:18:03.188
So this is like a fascinating track
of conversation talking about how

00:18:03.188 --> 00:18:07.545
you show up in a space and
how you exert influence.

00:18:07.545 --> 00:18:11.233
There are a couple questions here about,
and I'm sure this is going to be

00:18:11.233 --> 00:18:14.370
a recurring question that comes
up over our conversation.

00:18:14.370 --> 00:18:19.027
But from either touchy-feely,
acting with power or

00:18:19.027 --> 00:18:23.898
any of the other classes,
maybe even leadership labs.

00:18:23.898 --> 00:18:27.164
Have there been any instances where you've
been implementing some of the things

00:18:27.164 --> 00:18:28.412
you've learned in there, and

00:18:28.412 --> 00:18:32.346
how has that like affected the way
that you operate in the working place?

00:18:32.346 --> 00:18:33.430
&gt;&gt; All the time.

00:18:33.430 --> 00:18:38.727
I mean, especially for, I always joke for
better words, in product,

00:18:38.727 --> 00:18:43.936
I have not had a moment where I could
be alone in a room just doing stuff,

00:18:43.936 --> 00:18:48.200
which means that everything
is interpersonal, right?

00:18:48.200 --> 00:18:53.231
Everything is about how people
are reacting to the ideas

00:18:53.231 --> 00:19:00.522
that I'm bringing forth, how I'm reacting
to their input and collaboration.

00:19:00.522 --> 00:19:06.159
And so for me a few of the examples that
I think about are we learn about when

00:19:06.159 --> 00:19:12.373
to use emotion, for example, and how
to express different levels of emotion.

00:19:12.373 --> 00:19:17.502
And how someone can be even keeled for
the majority of their time,

00:19:17.502 --> 00:19:23.190
as when you know them all of a sudden
when they engage in extreme emotion,

00:19:23.190 --> 00:19:25.907
you really pay attention to that.

00:19:25.907 --> 00:19:30.520
Because it's so different,
then they're standard status quo, and so

00:19:30.520 --> 00:19:35.600
that's a tactic that I've used a few times
at work to really get a point across.

00:19:35.600 --> 00:19:39.653
It's not something that I
would choose to abuse but

00:19:39.653 --> 00:19:43.516
it's one that I remember
when I went through it,

00:19:43.516 --> 00:19:48.432
it was a kind of like an aha
finding moment from touchy-feely.

00:19:48.432 --> 00:19:52.424
People gave me feedback that said,
I was really surprised,

00:19:52.424 --> 00:19:56.114
when you act that way,
it really made me pay attention.

00:19:56.114 --> 00:20:01.389
And it was not something that I
did on purpose in touchy-feely

00:20:01.389 --> 00:20:06.666
because it happened to be a very
involuntary reaction to something

00:20:06.666 --> 00:20:12.180
that happened there but
was a really powerful learning.

00:20:12.180 --> 00:20:17.962
And then the other one is around how to,
I've already talked about,

00:20:17.962 --> 00:20:22.847
is around how to show,
kind of like use vulnerability to get

00:20:22.847 --> 00:20:27.054
people to understand you better and
to trust you.

00:20:27.054 --> 00:20:31.653
So I think that was one of, again,

00:20:31.653 --> 00:20:37.216
a huge lesson from these classes around.

00:20:37.216 --> 00:20:40.675
I think that the kind of,
I call it cheap learning,

00:20:40.675 --> 00:20:43.739
is to never show cracks in the armor,
right?

00:20:43.739 --> 00:20:51.857
To never waver from a strong determined
person who knows what they're doing.

00:20:51.857 --> 00:20:57.326
But actually there are many times where
showing just a little bit of vulnerability

00:20:57.326 --> 00:21:02.793
or a lot of vulnerability if it's with the
right audience or the right people who,

00:21:02.793 --> 00:21:07.870
that's what it takes to get them on
board can be incredibly powerful.

00:21:07.870 --> 00:21:11.946
The tricky thing is that it doesn't
work on everyone and it doesn't work at

00:21:11.946 --> 00:21:16.360
the right time, which is [LAUGH] one of
the frustrating learnings from the class.

00:21:16.360 --> 00:21:19.040
It's not a perfect playbook.

00:21:19.040 --> 00:21:24.718
There's a lot of kind of try and learn and
try and learn but these have definitely

00:21:24.718 --> 00:21:29.707
been important things for me as I've
gone back into the world the last

00:21:29.707 --> 00:21:35.420
three years to get my points across and
be a successful product manager.

00:21:35.420 --> 00:21:38.814
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I love how that comes back
to what you're talking about earlier

00:21:38.814 --> 00:21:41.380
about getting the tools
you need to succeed.

00:21:41.380 --> 00:21:44.802
And it's like, here's the tool and
then in that, try and iterate and

00:21:44.802 --> 00:21:47.542
kind of workshopping it over
the course of your career.

00:21:47.542 --> 00:21:49.666
Just like continuing to sharpen and

00:21:49.666 --> 00:21:54.080
kind of fine tune those tools to
the settings that you need it for.

00:21:54.080 --> 00:21:55.905
&gt;&gt; There's a lot of fine tuning.

00:21:55.905 --> 00:21:59.800
[LAUGH] Sometimes you miss a little bit,
but there's a lot of fine tuning.

00:21:59.800 --> 00:22:04.806
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's part of the process too,
how do we embrace, quote unquote,

00:22:04.806 --> 00:22:09.986
failure as opportunities to learn growth
mindset, that spirit of feedback.

00:22:09.986 --> 00:22:11.760
[LAUGH] Feedback is a gift.

00:22:11.760 --> 00:22:19.488
&gt;&gt; Absolutely, yeah, feedback is a gift is
something that you hear a lot at the GSB,

00:22:19.488 --> 00:22:25.760
and I think is something that can be
not fun to take both at the GSB and

00:22:25.760 --> 00:22:31.810
in the real world, but
it is a really powerful takeaway.

00:22:31.810 --> 00:22:36.395
And I think something that I
still sort of, Wil as you said,

00:22:36.395 --> 00:22:41.535
workshop and tinker with and
decide how to really take in.

00:22:41.535 --> 00:22:46.358
And it is helpful to not be defensive
about feedback that comes in, and

00:22:46.358 --> 00:22:52.029
that was a big, for me personally, it's
like a big learning during my time here.

00:22:52.029 --> 00:22:52.763
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I mean,

00:22:52.763 --> 00:22:56.444
it's a learning that I'm still trying
to process even in the working world.

00:22:56.444 --> 00:22:58.445
[LAUGH] It's just like I feel something.

00:22:58.445 --> 00:22:59.676
Let's unpack that.

00:22:59.676 --> 00:23:00.825
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Exactly.

00:23:02.310 --> 00:23:03.749
&gt;&gt; Let's switch over to the hard skills.

00:23:03.749 --> 00:23:07.250
I know we talked a lot about
the personal development,

00:23:07.250 --> 00:23:10.229
how you show up in a space,
how you influence.

00:23:10.229 --> 00:23:14.845
What are also like some of
the technical hard skills that you find

00:23:14.845 --> 00:23:16.653
are helpful in your job?

00:23:16.653 --> 00:23:20.964
Kind of whether it's in product itself, or
just kind of in the tech world writ large.

00:23:20.964 --> 00:23:23.912
because I know there are also
some questions here about,

00:23:23.912 --> 00:23:29.400
okay, what are other opportunities in the
world of tech beyond product management?

00:23:29.400 --> 00:23:32.670
&gt;&gt; Yeah, definitely,
a few classes that come to mind.

00:23:32.670 --> 00:23:37.749
For me the areas that were,
so I was a biochem major, so

00:23:37.749 --> 00:23:42.615
for me, academically,
a lot of this stuff was new.

00:23:42.615 --> 00:23:46.085
So there were a few marketing classes,
for example, that I found to be helpful.

00:23:46.085 --> 00:23:50.535
So one that comes to mind is
a class called consumer behavior.

00:23:51.685 --> 00:23:57.010
We really focused on,
I wouldn't call them tricks but

00:23:57.010 --> 00:24:03.274
actual tactics and
strategies to get consumers to better,

00:24:03.274 --> 00:24:06.617
Go towards your desired outcome,
let's put it that way.

00:24:06.617 --> 00:24:11.898
And so, I think that one in particular is
important if you're thinking about going

00:24:11.898 --> 00:24:17.193
to a B2C company, where your main user
will be a consumer but it's still helpful.

00:24:17.193 --> 00:24:21.777
Even for me at a company like Rippling
where I work today that is B2B,

00:24:21.777 --> 00:24:25.493
where most of my customers
are business customers, but

00:24:25.493 --> 00:24:29.620
it's small businesses, so
they tend to act like consumers.

00:24:29.620 --> 00:24:34.042
And so, when they think about things like
visual design and how we talk to them,

00:24:34.042 --> 00:24:36.959
I definitely think of some
lessons from that class.

00:24:36.959 --> 00:24:41.274
Honestly, my intro to marketing class
around things like segmentation,

00:24:41.274 --> 00:24:44.630
and positioning, and targeting,
was really critical,

00:24:44.630 --> 00:24:46.903
I didn't have any background in that.

00:24:46.903 --> 00:24:51.946
I definitely came from
the mindset that marketing was

00:24:51.946 --> 00:24:58.233
some voodoo art that didn't have
a lot of actual discipline to it.

00:24:58.233 --> 00:25:02.856
And so, that has been really helpful and
thinking about how you, like for me,

00:25:02.856 --> 00:25:06.359
at least as a product manager,
how I focus on my customers,

00:25:06.359 --> 00:25:09.184
how I segment them,
who my target customer is.

00:25:09.184 --> 00:25:15.592
How I can make sure that when I'm building
something or I'm prioritizing something,

00:25:15.592 --> 00:25:20.435
I'm really focused on what I've
refined as the target segment.

00:25:20.435 --> 00:25:23.991
And not just trying to peanut
butter it to an entire market,

00:25:23.991 --> 00:25:28.560
because that's impossible to solve,
and so that has been very important.

00:25:28.560 --> 00:25:34.543
And then there's a lot of, I had never
taken an accounting class to be honest.

00:25:34.543 --> 00:25:38.586
And actually some people will say,
accounting is boring, it's this and that.

00:25:38.586 --> 00:25:43.559
I think it's a really critical foundation
for anyone who's going to end up being

00:25:43.559 --> 00:25:48.001
a leader in business, even if you're
not going to be an accountant, and

00:25:48.001 --> 00:25:53.381
I don't think anyone in my class really
went deep into specializing in accounting.

00:25:53.381 --> 00:25:58.688
But it's a really critical core
skill to understand profit and

00:25:58.688 --> 00:26:03.902
loss statement, and cash flows,
and things of that nature.

00:26:03.902 --> 00:26:08.596
It makes you much more able to talk
when you're in a room with other folks,

00:26:08.596 --> 00:26:12.458
maybe finance folks that are trying
to make decisions around

00:26:12.458 --> 00:26:15.046
how to allocate head count to your team.

00:26:15.046 --> 00:26:17.395
And you're like,
I want to go toe-to-toe with you and

00:26:17.395 --> 00:26:19.704
make sure that I get the proper
head count that I need.

00:26:19.704 --> 00:26:23.943
You're able to do that a lot more
than someone who's just like,

00:26:23.943 --> 00:26:28.587
I'm only a product manager and
just put the heads on my team of people.

00:26:28.587 --> 00:26:32.982
So actually having a detailed
numbers-based conversation is incredibly

00:26:32.982 --> 00:26:33.615
helpful.

00:26:33.615 --> 00:26:38.423
And then, lastly, I mean, some other
classes that were very specific, not very

00:26:38.423 --> 00:26:43.032
specific, but ended up really helping
in product, so one is Startup Garage.

00:26:43.032 --> 00:26:48.038
As you know, Startup Garage
sort of employs design thinking

00:26:48.038 --> 00:26:54.530
to how you refine a problem, and then
eventually think through a hypothesis.

00:26:54.530 --> 00:27:00.295
And point of view statement in a solution
which even though Startup Garage sort of

00:27:00.295 --> 00:27:06.340
sounds like something that you would do if
you want to go only into entrepreneurship.

00:27:06.340 --> 00:27:12.762
That's actually not quite correct,
the frameworks that are taught there,

00:27:12.762 --> 00:27:18.392
I use pretty routinely in product,
because we really try in product

00:27:18.392 --> 00:27:24.447
to think about agile development and
hypothesis-based development.

00:27:24.447 --> 00:27:29.319
And so, this type of a class really forces
you to go through the rigor of refining

00:27:29.319 --> 00:27:32.010
a problem, asking a bajillion questions,

00:27:32.010 --> 00:27:36.252
lead the customer discovery before
you ever think about a solution.

00:27:36.252 --> 00:27:41.573
And so, that one, I actually don't
think was properly advertised.

00:27:41.573 --> 00:27:43.081
I remember when going through it,

00:27:43.081 --> 00:27:45.475
as this will help you if you
want to go into product.

00:27:45.475 --> 00:27:48.879
They were sort of like, hey,
people that are thinking about

00:27:48.879 --> 00:27:53.591
entrepreneurship go here, and looking
back on it, that one was really helpful.

00:27:53.591 --> 00:27:58.259
The last one that I talked about is called
aligning startups with their market,

00:27:58.259 --> 00:28:03.151
which actually don't know if it's still
being offered, but was really helpful.

00:28:03.151 --> 00:28:08.101
And again, even though aligning startups
with their markets sounds like it's really

00:28:08.101 --> 00:28:09.946
focused on entrepreneurship.

00:28:09.946 --> 00:28:12.570
It can be leveraged for, basically,

00:28:12.570 --> 00:28:17.740
any new product initiative even if it's
at a larger company in my opinion.

00:28:17.740 --> 00:28:23.515
And so, again, really focused on
customer discovery and how you pivot,

00:28:23.515 --> 00:28:28.173
how you judge when you may have
reached product market fit,

00:28:28.173 --> 00:28:30.521
how you think about scaling.

00:28:30.521 --> 00:28:34.271
So, those are a few of the classes that
come to mind, there are, obviously,

00:28:34.271 --> 00:28:37.176
a bunch of other ones that I
found to be helpful throughout.

00:28:37.176 --> 00:28:40.976
&gt;&gt; Yeah,
I love all of the translatable learnings,

00:28:40.976 --> 00:28:46.133
the frameworks and not just
entrepreneurship as a career path, but

00:28:46.133 --> 00:28:51.666
also as a way of thinking that is
applicable to so many different spaces.

00:28:51.666 --> 00:28:53.019
&gt;&gt; Exactly.

00:28:53.019 --> 00:28:57.699
&gt;&gt; So you've been at a large firm,
you've been at a small firm,

00:28:57.699 --> 00:29:03.729
you've seen a breadth of different kind
of roles that someone can go into tech,

00:29:03.729 --> 00:29:09.137
especially thinking about the MBA and
the skill sets that you develop.

00:29:09.137 --> 00:29:12.632
What are some other roles within
the tech world that you've seen some

00:29:12.632 --> 00:29:14.089
of your classmates go into?

00:29:14.089 --> 00:29:17.446
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so
there are a bunch of different roles.

00:29:17.446 --> 00:29:22.663
So one of the more common ones that I've
seen is business ops, which is depending

00:29:22.663 --> 00:29:27.361
on the company, it's sort of an internal
consultant at a tech company.

00:29:27.361 --> 00:29:32.529
[LAUGH] A second one would be
Product Marketing Management,

00:29:32.529 --> 00:29:35.743
PMM, very, very common to go into.

00:29:35.743 --> 00:29:40.691
And then there's sort of a whole host
of other more refined things at smaller

00:29:40.691 --> 00:29:44.883
companies or larger companies
that call them different stuff.

00:29:44.883 --> 00:29:47.216
So, they run the gamut, right?

00:29:47.216 --> 00:29:52.673
You can be on the non-development
side of the house and

00:29:52.673 --> 00:29:59.679
still definitely be in the tech world
having come from an MBA, right?

00:29:59.679 --> 00:30:05.643
You can deploy more strategic skills
purely or you can be on the product side,

00:30:05.643 --> 00:30:09.329
so I've seen people go
into a variety of roles.

00:30:09.329 --> 00:30:14.273
In my opinion, that's one of
the beauties of kind of a general

00:30:14.273 --> 00:30:18.945
management MBA,
purposefully makes you very valuable.

00:30:18.945 --> 00:30:22.337
One of the frustrating things about it
could be that it doesn't set you on

00:30:22.337 --> 00:30:23.343
a super narrow path.

00:30:23.343 --> 00:30:27.297
So, I think that's a really
important consideration for

00:30:27.297 --> 00:30:32.222
folks who are thinking about a full-time
general management MBA to know

00:30:32.222 --> 00:30:34.737
it's sort of opens up a lot of doors.

00:30:34.737 --> 00:30:38.614
But it will force you to
do a lot of research and

00:30:38.614 --> 00:30:44.241
testing to see which of the many
opportunities are right for you.

00:30:44.241 --> 00:30:46.207
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and then pivoting, right?

00:30:46.207 --> 00:30:47.758
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Post-graduation,

00:30:47.758 --> 00:30:51.876
that's probably not going to be your
career for the rest of your life.

00:30:51.876 --> 00:30:52.539
&gt;&gt; 100%.
&gt;&gt; And so

00:30:52.539 --> 00:30:56.516
how do you set up for
the skills to thrive elsewhere?

00:30:56.516 --> 00:31:00.124
because you've already pivoted,
right, from large to small.

00:31:00.124 --> 00:31:03.583
&gt;&gt; That's right, yeah, and I think for me,

00:31:03.583 --> 00:31:07.424
actually one of the main
sort of the last push for

00:31:07.424 --> 00:31:13.791
me to end up actually applying for an MBA,
was because I had gotten to point at.

00:31:13.791 --> 00:31:17.887
So I worked at a company called Zenefits
prior to the GSB, and I had been there for

00:31:17.887 --> 00:31:20.964
a year, I had done some other
stuff in a different company.

00:31:20.964 --> 00:31:24.578
But I had been there for a year,
I felt like I was doing really well,

00:31:24.578 --> 00:31:26.590
folks really appreciating my work.

00:31:26.590 --> 00:31:30.560
And I decided that I wanted to switch
to a different team internally,

00:31:30.560 --> 00:31:34.678
actually wanted to switch from
implementation to product marketing.

00:31:34.678 --> 00:31:38.597
And so, I went through a pretty
disorganized internal process,

00:31:38.597 --> 00:31:41.740
as a small company,
there's no official process.

00:31:41.740 --> 00:31:45.752
I spoke with the person who
was running that team, and

00:31:45.752 --> 00:31:50.047
she ended up giving me a little
case exercise, I solved.

00:31:50.047 --> 00:31:52.826
And she's like, you're great,
we'd love to have you join.

00:31:52.826 --> 00:31:57.340
And eventually what happened was, and so
I was like, that's amazing, I'm so excited

00:31:57.340 --> 00:32:00.818
about this new opportunity, because
I was tired of where I was working.

00:32:00.818 --> 00:32:03.600
I wanted to learn new skills,
and they ended up for

00:32:03.600 --> 00:32:06.290
a certain amount of time getting.

00:32:06.290 --> 00:32:09.871
Blocked, and the thing that they told me
was you're doing well, where you are.

00:32:09.871 --> 00:32:12.901
You're doing very well where you are,
we need you here.

00:32:12.901 --> 00:32:19.190
And for me personally, I was at a prayer
I was like, no, I want to do this.

00:32:19.190 --> 00:32:24.420
And that was actually the thing that
prompted me to apply for an MBA.

00:32:24.420 --> 00:32:30.282
I ended up seeing an MBA as a way to make
sure that my career was future proof.

00:32:30.282 --> 00:32:37.181
It does not necessarily mean you're like
super successful day one high flying.

00:32:37.181 --> 00:32:41.468
But it does give you the skills
to have a really fascinating and

00:32:41.468 --> 00:32:44.774
dynamic career,
in my opinion, for decades.

00:32:44.774 --> 00:32:50.605
It gives you the general skills to be able
to pivot and not pigeonhole yourself.

00:32:50.605 --> 00:32:55.223
And so for me, with the point
that I was there I was like, no,

00:32:55.223 --> 00:33:00.130
I don't want to be told that this
is the appropriate path for you.

00:33:00.130 --> 00:33:02.298
Like I want to set out on my own path.

00:33:02.298 --> 00:33:05.069
But i want to make sure I have
kind of the two kit to derive.

00:33:05.069 --> 00:33:09.016
And so for me like that MBA
experience I was part of that.

00:33:09.016 --> 00:33:16.077
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's ow-full I know i get
to ask all the type of people like.

00:33:16.077 --> 00:33:20.013
How do you know its the right time or
even like?

00:33:20.013 --> 00:33:23.818
I don't know if I need
an MBA to be successful or

00:33:23.818 --> 00:33:26.170
thrive in in X, Y or Z field.

00:33:26.170 --> 00:33:27.954
So like, why go?

00:33:27.954 --> 00:33:29.873
And so even again.

00:33:29.873 --> 00:33:32.430
&gt;&gt; I think it's a super good question.

00:33:32.430 --> 00:33:34.721
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so for me,
I thought about it as a long game.

00:33:34.721 --> 00:33:37.931
I think it's a really,
it's a really good question, right?

00:33:37.931 --> 00:33:40.577
Like, for a lot of folks, they're like,

00:33:40.577 --> 00:33:43.896
I'm kicking butt at my
current place like I'm good.

00:33:43.896 --> 00:33:47.908
I don't need an out and, thinking
back to a lot of my classmates like,

00:33:47.908 --> 00:33:51.385
that's where they were,
they were doing incredibly well.

00:33:51.385 --> 00:33:57.143
And they still decided to take that
time in the way that I well it's funny.

00:33:57.143 --> 00:34:01.421
When I was applying, I went actually
to a GSB admission session.

00:34:01.421 --> 00:34:06.600
And the director of admissions at
the time Derek started said this

00:34:06.600 --> 00:34:11.414
thing which again,
you kind of you take with a grain of salt.

00:34:11.414 --> 00:34:13.921
Because of course he's
trying to recruit people.

00:34:13.921 --> 00:34:17.456
But he said, it's never going to
be truly feel comfortable and

00:34:17.456 --> 00:34:20.030
never truly going to
feel like the right time.

00:34:20.030 --> 00:34:22.965
The goal it's always going to be
a bit of an uncomfortable decision.

00:34:22.965 --> 00:34:24.506
And if it is uncomfortable,

00:34:24.506 --> 00:34:28.177
a slightly uncomfortable decision
it's probably the right time.

00:34:28.177 --> 00:34:30.500
And for some reason that actually
really resonated with me.

00:34:30.500 --> 00:34:35.095
It's like, when if you wait until
the point where you feel like you've

00:34:35.095 --> 00:34:38.096
stagnated in your career and
then you need it.

00:34:38.096 --> 00:34:41.137
You probably waited just
a little bit too long,

00:34:41.137 --> 00:34:44.721
which I get is that it's
a very uncomfortable feeling.

00:34:44.721 --> 00:34:51.274
Obviously an MBA is not the right
thing for everyone at all.

00:34:51.274 --> 00:34:55.163
That I find it hard to believe that
you'll be at a point where you're like,

00:34:55.163 --> 00:34:57.773
this is the perfect point for
me to go now, right?

00:34:57.773 --> 00:35:00.070
Like, there's always a reason.

00:35:00.070 --> 00:35:00.826
Why not?

00:35:00.826 --> 00:35:02.400
There's always a reason to go elsewhere.

00:35:02.400 --> 00:35:05.119
So you really have to ask yourself, like.

00:35:05.119 --> 00:35:06.132
Why would I go?

00:35:06.132 --> 00:35:07.893
Wat's the value that I would get?

00:35:07.893 --> 00:35:08.509
What am i trading off?

00:35:08.509 --> 00:35:12.832
And have a really serious
conversation with yourself.

00:35:12.832 --> 00:35:17.271
And your stakeholders around in
your life around like when I put

00:35:17.271 --> 00:35:19.293
everything down on a paper.

00:35:19.293 --> 00:35:22.220
Or my mental checklist, or
however you make decisions, is this right?

00:35:22.220 --> 00:35:29.095
But I don't, it'll never be like a binary
obvious decision, that was the right time.

00:35:29.095 --> 00:35:34.054
That was the right school I think it's
difficult to get to that sort of clarity,

00:35:34.054 --> 00:35:35.098
at least for me.

00:35:35.098 --> 00:35:39.845
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so let's sit down with your
younger self and have that conversation.

00:35:39.845 --> 00:35:44.880
I didn't, you probably didn't think you're
going to be having this conversation.

00:35:44.880 --> 00:35:49.801
Say that this is a question from Danny
about, like, what was the value add for

00:35:49.801 --> 00:35:51.250
you after six months?

00:35:51.250 --> 00:35:56.605
So now that you're thinking like,
younger you.

00:35:56.605 --> 00:35:58.057
You from a pre-MBA.

00:35:58.057 --> 00:36:03.588
Where you're putting that decision,
how am I going to think about its worth,

00:36:03.588 --> 00:36:06.972
to you today, six months or
just afterwards.

00:36:06.972 --> 00:36:11.970
How would you articulate that to
your younger self to help them come

00:36:11.970 --> 00:36:13.140
to a decision?

00:36:13.140 --> 00:36:14.700
&gt;&gt; Yeah, it's a good question.

00:36:14.700 --> 00:36:19.711
I think the way that I look at it now,
it kind of, it goes back to that.

00:36:19.711 --> 00:36:21.070
At least for me,

00:36:21.070 --> 00:36:26.711
I had always seen my MBA as a long-term
investment in my future self.

00:36:26.711 --> 00:36:32.860
So it was not something that I expected
that the day after graduation would be.

00:36:32.860 --> 00:36:37.566
My gosh, I turned the page and
like everything is different.

00:36:37.566 --> 00:36:42.863
And it's one of those things, at least for
me where I see the lessons in like,

00:36:42.863 --> 00:36:47.853
small bits throughout my day like
thinking back to the last three years.

00:36:47.853 --> 00:36:52.220
That consistently throughout
the last three years.

00:36:52.220 --> 00:36:55.852
And I anticipate that I will keep
seeing that as time goes on.

00:36:55.852 --> 00:36:58.776
And so a part of it definitely like,

00:36:58.776 --> 00:37:02.677
it did help me get to
the job at a larger company.

00:37:02.677 --> 00:37:05.440
That I was interested in
pursuing right after the GSB.

00:37:05.440 --> 00:37:08.990
So that's kind of like a very
tangible thing that it did.

00:37:08.990 --> 00:37:13.806
But I think more importantly,
what it enabled me to do is to have that

00:37:13.806 --> 00:37:18.548
flexibility to be able to be in
a variety of different situations.

00:37:18.548 --> 00:37:23.507
That I you it's hard to anticipate
being in me like I did sort of going

00:37:23.507 --> 00:37:28.127
through a laboratory [LAUGH] that
helped me prepare me for that.

00:37:28.127 --> 00:37:32.281
Or I did have a classmate
who worked in that industry.

00:37:32.281 --> 00:37:37.052
Or I did have like this really rich
conversation with someone about this

00:37:37.052 --> 00:37:41.362
particular problem that they had and
now I'm going through it.

00:37:41.362 --> 00:37:45.972
Or I need to form a partnership for
work and hey,

00:37:45.972 --> 00:37:49.442
this other GSB is on the other side.

00:37:49.442 --> 00:37:55.439
So like, you sort of see the value
in like a variety of different ways,

00:37:55.439 --> 00:37:57.075
ways throughout.

00:37:57.075 --> 00:37:59.849
&gt;&gt; Nice, and then there's a question here.

00:37:59.849 --> 00:38:02.966
So, actually multiple
questions around like.

00:38:02.966 --> 00:38:07.656
Hey, I'm going to distill it and this is
these are questions from Ryan and Addy and

00:38:07.656 --> 00:38:09.617
Jinzhou, about Kinsale about.

00:38:09.617 --> 00:38:13.037
We talked a little bit about classes,
but for

00:38:13.037 --> 00:38:17.454
folks who are like thinking
about pivoting or exploring.

00:38:17.454 --> 00:38:21.622
What are some opportunities and
resources that can help you get at least

00:38:21.622 --> 00:38:24.759
a little bit of expert like
experience in that world?

00:38:24.759 --> 00:38:27.842
And kind of get your feet wet?

00:38:27.842 --> 00:38:32.013
&gt;&gt; I assume that they're asking while
at the GSB because obviously like some

00:38:32.013 --> 00:38:32.557
people.

00:38:32.557 --> 00:38:36.070
So I think it was,
I can think of a few things.

00:38:36.070 --> 00:38:38.232
Some of my classmates, for example,

00:38:38.232 --> 00:38:41.418
decided to take classes what
we call across the street.

00:38:41.418 --> 00:38:43.458
Which is outside of the GSB
at other Stanford classes.

00:38:43.458 --> 00:38:48.304
So some people decided to take classes
at the design school for example.

00:38:48.304 --> 00:38:51.310
Send people to classes in
the computer science department.

00:38:51.310 --> 00:38:53.485
God bless them.

00:38:53.485 --> 00:38:57.755
There are a whole bunch of things that
that being at a school like Stanford

00:38:57.755 --> 00:38:58.807
allows you to do.

00:38:58.807 --> 00:38:59.750
So that's one.

00:38:59.750 --> 00:39:06.507
I think the second thing is doing
a part time during the year internship.

00:39:06.507 --> 00:39:09.109
Which again is a sacrifice and
a trade off.

00:39:09.109 --> 00:39:13.962
But part of being in product
is all about trade offs.

00:39:13.962 --> 00:39:17.202
And to practice that in real life.

00:39:17.202 --> 00:39:19.140
So I think that's incredibly helpful.

00:39:19.140 --> 00:39:23.934
I think growing to really like
tapping into the alumni network is

00:39:23.934 --> 00:39:25.721
critical from day one.

00:39:25.721 --> 00:39:28.689
And it's a lot of work like, networking.

00:39:28.689 --> 00:39:32.404
Some people love networking,
other people despise networking.

00:39:32.404 --> 00:39:35.510
But no matter how you look at it,
it's incredibly time consuming.

00:39:35.510 --> 00:39:39.054
I think it's rewarding and fun, but
like it's really time consuming.

00:39:39.054 --> 00:39:43.983
So being able to spend a bunch of
your time, having conversations with

00:39:43.983 --> 00:39:48.672
people in different industries,
at different companies sizes.

00:39:48.672 --> 00:39:53.545
Because one of the beauties and
frustrating things about product is

00:39:53.545 --> 00:39:57.402
that when you ask people
what a product manager does.

00:39:57.402 --> 00:39:59.979
It, the answer will range from A to Z,

00:39:59.979 --> 00:40:03.695
especially if you ask people
at different companies.

00:40:03.695 --> 00:40:07.482
And so it's actually really important
to be able to do the research,

00:40:07.482 --> 00:40:09.320
to understand like.

00:40:09.320 --> 00:40:11.480
Okay, is this someone
who's doing more strategy?

00:40:11.480 --> 00:40:15.238
Are they spending 80% of their time
with just engineering and design?

00:40:15.238 --> 00:40:17.120
Are they more on the go to market side?

00:40:17.120 --> 00:40:21.698
And how does that fit my strengths and
my desires and

00:40:21.698 --> 00:40:26.382
how can I use that to refine
my career search better so

00:40:26.382 --> 00:40:30.536
that I can achieve both
my desired outcomes but

00:40:30.536 --> 00:40:35.464
what I'm capable of doing
given my prior background.

00:40:35.464 --> 00:40:40.018
So I would do a combo of those, right,
like talk to alumni outside of classes,

00:40:40.018 --> 00:40:42.175
talk to a bunch of your classmates.

00:40:42.175 --> 00:40:47.162
A lot of my classmates actually sort
of we joke at the GC that everyone

00:40:47.162 --> 00:40:48.658
has a side hustles.

00:40:48.658 --> 00:40:52.933
So a lot of people tried to start
even outside of startup garage,

00:40:52.933 --> 00:40:57.841
they have little things that they were
trying out and hey, that's the way

00:40:57.841 --> 00:41:02.772
to both have real world experience,
but also to beef up your resume a bit.

00:41:02.772 --> 00:41:07.779
So, and the other thing that folks did,
especially when they were trying to

00:41:07.779 --> 00:41:13.101
transition is they did either incredibly
low paid or unpaid internships which,

00:41:13.101 --> 00:41:17.970
at smaller companies to at least get
a sense of what product would be like.

00:41:17.970 --> 00:41:22.960
We had no experience and
you can do that while you're in school.

00:41:22.960 --> 00:41:28.209
It's not as big of a sacrifice as
obviously doing something unpaid

00:41:28.209 --> 00:41:33.472
post graduation, which is a really
big decision and investment.

00:41:33.472 --> 00:41:36.706
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I'm going to go back to
something you said a little bit earlier

00:41:36.706 --> 00:41:39.421
about spending a lot of time and
energy in networking and

00:41:39.421 --> 00:41:42.855
how some people thrive in it and
others it's a little bit more effort.

00:41:44.125 --> 00:41:49.080
Having gone through that process yourself
and kind of lessons learned along the way.

00:41:49.080 --> 00:41:53.215
For those of us who find it a little
bit more draining, any tips or

00:41:53.215 --> 00:41:58.117
inspiration or words of encouragement
that you have to help us think about,

00:41:58.117 --> 00:42:03.240
what is networking, how can we apply
it especially in the tech space?

00:42:03.240 --> 00:42:04.146
&gt;&gt; I would think about it.

00:42:04.146 --> 00:42:08.127
Some people approach networking as like,
so

00:42:08.127 --> 00:42:12.851
I would say quality is
better than quantity, right?

00:42:12.851 --> 00:42:17.678
So, if you're thinking about
networking as like, if I have 50,

00:42:17.678 --> 00:42:22.760
5-0 conversations and they're each,
boom, boom, boom, boom,

00:42:22.760 --> 00:42:28.882
boom, kind of 15 minutes very focused,
I'm sure I'll get something out of them.

00:42:28.882 --> 00:42:32.105
I mean, yeah, you might but
it sounds really painful.

00:42:32.105 --> 00:42:34.909
You have to be a very particular
sort of person to do that.

00:42:34.909 --> 00:42:40.521
I would focus on quality so
spending more time doing more research,

00:42:40.521 --> 00:42:45.642
but having less conversations
actually would be my advice for

00:42:45.642 --> 00:42:50.073
the majority of the time to
invest in a conversation,

00:42:50.073 --> 00:42:54.125
really go deep with someone
who's a proper fit.

00:42:54.125 --> 00:42:59.147
But then obviously yeah,
part of it is sure it's a numbers game but

00:42:59.147 --> 00:43:02.554
that I would more tailor
towards keep an eye,

00:43:02.554 --> 00:43:05.977
have quick conversations with classmates.

00:43:05.977 --> 00:43:11.205
Even if y'all aren't best friends or you
don't necessarily see the right path but

00:43:11.205 --> 00:43:13.904
choose where you're investing the time.

00:43:13.904 --> 00:43:16.155
Choose which alumni
you're reaching out to.

00:43:16.155 --> 00:43:18.552
Don't run yourself dry.

00:43:18.552 --> 00:43:24.147
I think networking will be a lot less
painful if you're having conversations

00:43:24.147 --> 00:43:29.150
with with people that you truly find
to be valuable and interesting.

00:43:29.150 --> 00:43:31.850
I don't think it'll be 100% of the time.

00:43:31.850 --> 00:43:35.751
Sometimes it's like, yeah,
it's a bit of work and it's a grind.

00:43:35.751 --> 00:43:40.266
But I think if most of the networking
that you do is stuff that you find to be

00:43:40.266 --> 00:43:44.499
interesting, which will probably
mean just less conversations.

00:43:44.499 --> 00:43:49.342
You're going to be, okay, that maybe took
me a little bit to click send on an email.

00:43:49.342 --> 00:43:53.436
It took me a little bit to get out of
the house to go to Koopa in the middle of

00:43:53.436 --> 00:43:55.416
campus even though I was like God,

00:43:55.416 --> 00:43:58.640
I really don't want to do
the awkward coffee meetup.

00:43:58.640 --> 00:44:03.400
But otherwise, I feel once you sit down
and have the conversation, it's really

00:44:03.400 --> 00:44:08.480
rewarding, and I guess now in the world
of COVID it'll probably be via Zoom.

00:44:08.480 --> 00:44:10.280
But you get my point.

00:44:10.280 --> 00:44:16.060
&gt;&gt; Yeah, here's a question from Sofia,
that has a number of upvotes.

00:44:16.060 --> 00:44:21.100
What surprised you about the PM role or
experience coming out of the GSB?

00:44:21.100 --> 00:44:26.671
And we talked a little bit about, actually
a lot about how the GSB set you up for

00:44:26.671 --> 00:44:30.340
success, but yeah, any surprises?

00:44:30.340 --> 00:44:35.577
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I mean, so at least for me,
right, the role that I took after the GSB

00:44:35.577 --> 00:44:40.748
was at PayPal, which is not massive
like Google, but it's quite large.

00:44:40.748 --> 00:44:46.981
It was not a total shocker because again,
I interned there,

00:44:46.981 --> 00:44:51.993
but what,
the just the sheer amount of time that I

00:44:51.993 --> 00:44:57.372
spent sort of convincing
people of stuff rather than

00:44:57.372 --> 00:45:02.404
solving a problem or
actually building things.

00:45:02.404 --> 00:45:06.442
That was a bit of a surprise and
a bit of a disappointment and

00:45:06.442 --> 00:45:11.966
I think it might have been a function of
the particular team that I went on, which

00:45:11.966 --> 00:45:17.842
was more of a newer team at a big company,
which was really exciting for me actually.

00:45:17.842 --> 00:45:22.605
But then I think looking back on it and
then that it was a lot of

00:45:22.605 --> 00:45:27.900
trying to get people on board
with really unbaked ideas.

00:45:27.900 --> 00:45:30.961
And so for me learning from that was okay,

00:45:30.961 --> 00:45:36.030
I should try to, I get a lot of
energy out of seeing impact.

00:45:36.030 --> 00:45:39.524
Which I don't think is a rare thing,
but at least for

00:45:39.524 --> 00:45:43.503
me that has a very concrete,
positive reinforcing cycle.

00:45:43.503 --> 00:45:47.938
And so that was one of the reasons
why I decided to go to my

00:45:47.938 --> 00:45:52.951
current company to Ripling which is and
was a lot smaller, but

00:45:52.951 --> 00:45:57.967
fast growing in it is much easier for
me to see the impact that my

00:45:57.967 --> 00:46:03.191
work is doing technically in
the same role as a product manager.

00:46:03.191 --> 00:46:08.077
Which is day in and
day out I'm able to ship little things and

00:46:08.077 --> 00:46:12.573
or big things and
see how our customers are using them,

00:46:12.573 --> 00:46:17.593
see the pleasure or
pain that they're giving to customers.

00:46:17.593 --> 00:46:22.911
And for me, that's a huge, huge,
huge, positive reinforcement.

00:46:22.911 --> 00:46:25.990
So I'm like, ooh, I want more of that.

00:46:25.990 --> 00:46:27.127
I will work harder.

00:46:27.127 --> 00:46:30.535
Whereas when I, at least for
me when I was at PayPal,

00:46:30.535 --> 00:46:34.408
I just spent a ton of time
doing PowerPoint presentations,

00:46:34.408 --> 00:46:37.600
[LAUGH] which I knew was
going to be a part of the role.

00:46:37.600 --> 00:46:42.517
I did not anticipate that it would
consume that proportion of as

00:46:42.517 --> 00:46:45.690
high of a proportion of my time as it did.

00:46:45.690 --> 00:46:50.025
&gt;&gt; Yeah, it sounds like in any
presentation some of those soft skills,

00:46:50.025 --> 00:46:55.723
interpersonal dynamics, learnings, acting
with power probably came in really handy.

00:46:55.723 --> 00:46:56.545
&gt;&gt; Absolutely, yeah.

00:46:56.545 --> 00:47:00.122
So yeah, so that was helpful,
I was able to navigate it well.

00:47:00.122 --> 00:47:04.290
I just had gotten to a point where I
said okay, do I want to spend 80%?

00:47:04.290 --> 00:47:08.178
Do I want to spend 20% of my time
kind of talking in between teams and

00:47:08.178 --> 00:47:11.542
doing presentations or
do I want to spend 80% of my time?

00:47:11.542 --> 00:47:16.027
And so at least for me that decision
to move to Ripling at least at this

00:47:16.027 --> 00:47:20.920
very fine tuned stage in my career,
I was like, okay, I want to shift it.

00:47:20.920 --> 00:47:21.896
I'm very realistic.

00:47:21.896 --> 00:47:28.050
I understand that inner dynamics
at work are critical to succeed.

00:47:28.050 --> 00:47:29.457
That's one of my learnings from the GSB.

00:47:29.457 --> 00:47:33.358
But I would prefer to optimize for

00:47:33.358 --> 00:47:37.686
less of my time being spent that way.

00:47:37.686 --> 00:47:40.772
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and here's a question
from David that I really,

00:47:40.772 --> 00:47:43.540
this is the first time
I've heard this question.

00:47:43.540 --> 00:47:49.154
I really appreciate it because we talk
a lot about spirit of collaboration,

00:47:49.154 --> 00:47:50.128
community.

00:47:50.128 --> 00:47:54.536
But David wants to know what opportunities
are available for GSB students

00:47:54.536 --> 00:47:59.330
interested in tech or product to connect
and or work on projects together?

00:47:59.330 --> 00:48:03.274
We talked a lot about supporting each
other but on a more tactical side,

00:48:03.274 --> 00:48:04.205
does it happen?

00:48:04.205 --> 00:48:07.722
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's a great, so
I mean, it definitely happened so

00:48:07.722 --> 00:48:12.203
immediately when you ask that question
startup garage comes to mind, right?

00:48:12.203 --> 00:48:16.721
That's a very Tangible way
to point out the way this

00:48:16.721 --> 00:48:22.250
enterprise works is that you end up
forming teams of four to five people.

00:48:22.250 --> 00:48:29.052
And you propose a high level problem
statement and you form these teams.

00:48:29.052 --> 00:48:33.974
And so that's probably one of
the more concrete ways that the GSB

00:48:33.974 --> 00:48:35.532
facilitates that.

00:48:35.532 --> 00:48:36.397
That said,

00:48:36.397 --> 00:48:41.601
there are a bunch of ad hoc ways that
people end up sort of collaborating.

00:48:41.601 --> 00:48:48.321
There are things like career groups your
first year, where you get put together and

00:48:48.321 --> 00:48:54.570
you have a facilitator from the CMC,
where you talk about this sort of stuff.

00:48:54.570 --> 00:48:58.424
So I say that there's more
tactical hands on things.

00:48:58.424 --> 00:49:03.635
And actually a lot of your classes
end up having group based projects,

00:49:03.635 --> 00:49:07.979
which is [LAUGH] a double edged sword,
but a good learning.

00:49:07.979 --> 00:49:10.097
So it'll sort of depend, right?

00:49:10.097 --> 00:49:14.638
If I saw a bunch of folks from my class
who ended up either starting side

00:49:14.638 --> 00:49:19.573
hustles and or real companies together
throughout their times at the GSB,

00:49:19.573 --> 00:49:23.010
some of them are still
working on these companies.

00:49:23.010 --> 00:49:26.535
And most of them were not start up garage.

00:49:26.535 --> 00:49:29.820
It was like, hey I'm interested in this,
hey I'm interested in this.

00:49:29.820 --> 00:49:32.772
Hey, we either have the same background or

00:49:32.772 --> 00:49:37.942
we have complementary backgrounds,
let's try to work on this together.

00:49:37.942 --> 00:49:44.350
Or what we joke about, the classic, try
to recruit a CS student to work with you.

00:49:44.350 --> 00:49:46.281
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] I saw a little bit of that

00:49:46.281 --> 00:49:50.523
trying to happen, but
hopefully we can dispel those stereotypes.

00:49:50.523 --> 00:49:55.075
I think GSBers can can do their own
work without recruiting poor CS

00:49:55.075 --> 00:49:57.158
undergrads from [INAUDIBLE].

00:49:57.158 --> 00:49:59.915
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] But there is a lot of
interaction with other parts of

00:49:59.915 --> 00:50:00.956
the campus, right?

00:50:00.956 --> 00:50:01.884
&gt;&gt; Absolutely.

00:50:01.884 --> 00:50:04.711
&gt;&gt; The different d.school courses.

00:50:04.711 --> 00:50:09.701
&gt;&gt; I was going to say d.school courses,
bio design is a good one where

00:50:09.701 --> 00:50:14.151
they end up bringing folks
from the engineering school.

00:50:14.151 --> 00:50:16.717
And I think from the d.school and
from the GSB.

00:50:16.717 --> 00:50:20.910
So that it kind of manifests
itself in a few different ways.

00:50:20.910 --> 00:50:23.598
&gt;&gt; Yeah, there's a number of
interdisciplinary courses that

00:50:23.598 --> 00:50:24.342
folks can take.

00:50:24.342 --> 00:50:28.370
Or like you said, taking classes
from other departments on campus.

00:50:28.370 --> 00:50:33.890
Great way to broaden your horizons both
intellectually and network-wise as well.

00:50:33.890 --> 00:50:35.050
&gt;&gt; Absolutely.

00:50:35.050 --> 00:50:38.920
&gt;&gt; All right, and so I'm going to be
selfish and take this last question.

00:50:40.700 --> 00:50:45.840
I'm curious, we actually haven't talked
about the application much at all.

00:50:45.840 --> 00:50:46.560
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:50:46.560 --> 00:50:51.114
&gt;&gt; And even though you don't know and
you probably never will know why

00:50:51.114 --> 00:50:55.099
you were admitted,
even though you are pretty awesome and

00:50:55.099 --> 00:50:58.535
I loved spending this
last 55 minutes with you.

00:50:58.535 --> 00:51:04.221
If you could give your younger self any
advice about the application process,

00:51:04.221 --> 00:51:06.470
what would you tell yourself?

00:51:07.660 --> 00:51:11.229
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so I mean, as you say, I think
one of the things that I joke about is,

00:51:11.229 --> 00:51:12.704
after people get into the GSB,

00:51:12.704 --> 00:51:15.458
they have all these fancy
theories about why they got in.

00:51:15.458 --> 00:51:19.949
No one knows why they got in, unless you
tapped into the admissions office's files.

00:51:19.949 --> 00:51:23.997
So I think for me, though,
what I would say is that,

00:51:23.997 --> 00:51:28.139
what I would tell myself
looking back at the process,

00:51:28.139 --> 00:51:32.758
is to try to enjoy it,
which sounds really tongue in cheek.

00:51:32.758 --> 00:51:37.505
But it is a rare time,
at least for me, I was like,

00:51:37.505 --> 00:51:41.236
heads down and working a full time job,

00:51:41.236 --> 00:51:47.002
as is I assume the vast majority
of the folks on this call are,

00:51:47.002 --> 00:51:50.872
or having a life and your personal life.

00:51:50.872 --> 00:51:55.087
And I remember at the time when I
was applying, I was like, my gosh,

00:51:55.087 --> 00:52:00.028
I have to think about what matters most to
me and why, you have got to be kidding me.

00:52:00.028 --> 00:52:04.459
But it really forces you to have a unique

00:52:04.459 --> 00:52:09.030
retrospective moment about yourself.

00:52:09.030 --> 00:52:14.704
Why is it that you're looking to
invest two years of your life and

00:52:14.704 --> 00:52:19.236
not an insignificant amount
of money in a program?

00:52:19.236 --> 00:52:23.172
And so, it's a rare opportunity
to be able to be introspective,

00:52:23.172 --> 00:52:27.191
where there's so many other things
happening around you in life.

00:52:27.191 --> 00:52:32.428
And it can be if you do spend
the time really going deep and

00:52:32.428 --> 00:52:37.860
thinking about okay,
why is this the right time for me?

00:52:37.860 --> 00:52:39.300
What do I want to get out of this?

00:52:40.920 --> 00:52:42.720
Why is it that I think it'll be helpful?

00:52:42.720 --> 00:52:43.963
What do I want to do after?

00:52:43.963 --> 00:52:48.470
It is an incredibly rewarding
introspective thing,

00:52:48.470 --> 00:52:51.480
probably even if you don't get in.

00:52:51.480 --> 00:52:56.141
[LAUGH] And I remember there's the GSB
grad from 2016 who was telling me

00:52:56.141 --> 00:52:57.805
this when I was applying.

00:52:57.805 --> 00:53:02.717
And I do remember being like, yeah,
easy for you to say because you got in,

00:53:02.717 --> 00:53:07.815
and she was in MBA too at the time,
but I don't know, it's really true.

00:53:07.815 --> 00:53:10.313
And the other thing that I would say is,

00:53:10.313 --> 00:53:13.655
no one else can come up with
the right answer for you.

00:53:13.655 --> 00:53:19.716
Because the combination of things
around why it's the right time,

00:53:19.716 --> 00:53:22.915
what you want to do with it, why you?

00:53:22.915 --> 00:53:28.615
You can't fake, that answer has to
come from investing the time and

00:53:28.615 --> 00:53:31.819
thinking about all those questions.

00:53:31.819 --> 00:53:36.260
And the story will inevitably
be uniquely for yourself.

00:53:36.260 --> 00:53:39.321
So I would tell myself
to enjoy the process,

00:53:39.321 --> 00:53:43.228
despite it being rightfully,
incredibly stressful.

00:53:43.228 --> 00:53:46.064
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Thank you so much for

00:53:46.064 --> 00:53:48.832
your wisdom sharing your experiences.

00:53:48.832 --> 00:53:51.595
Speaking of the application, next week,

00:53:51.595 --> 00:53:55.446
we are holding a series of
events themed application week.

00:53:55.446 --> 00:54:00.110
Where we are actually going to go into all
different facets of telling your story.

00:54:00.110 --> 00:54:03.802
What does your story mean in
a business school application context?

00:54:03.802 --> 00:54:05.800
What are ways to start it?

00:54:05.800 --> 00:54:07.110
How do you put pen to paper?

00:54:07.110 --> 00:54:09.980
How do you convey it to
people over kind of speaking?

00:54:09.980 --> 00:54:13.287
And we also have a number of different
panels with admission officers about

00:54:13.287 --> 00:54:14.960
the parts of the application.

00:54:14.960 --> 00:54:16.030
How do we use it?

00:54:16.030 --> 00:54:20.928
What are ways that you can think about
understanding who you are in our process

00:54:20.928 --> 00:54:23.870
and how to put your best foot forward.

00:54:23.870 --> 00:54:26.292
And if you haven't done so already,

00:54:26.292 --> 00:54:31.618
I encourage you to start MyStanford.mba
dashboard with a few questions there.

00:54:31.618 --> 00:54:32.837
We'll help consolidate and

00:54:32.837 --> 00:54:35.860
put together things that might
be of most interest to you.

00:54:35.860 --> 00:54:41.090
So with that, thank you again,
Tomar, I had [LAUGH] a lot of fun.

00:54:41.090 --> 00:54:45.680
I can't wait to see you on campus,
hopefully in the near future.

00:54:45.680 --> 00:54:49.830
Everybody who joined us, thank you for
sharing your time with us.

00:54:49.830 --> 00:54:52.677
Have a great rest of your day and
stay safe.

00:54:52.677 --> 00:54:57.652
[MUSIC]

